      <sourceDesc xml:id="CGis_sDesc_EN">
         <msDesc xml:id="EN - Codex Gissensis">
            <msIdentifier>
               <country>Germany</country>
               <settlement>Giessen</settlement>
               <repository>University Library</repository>
               <idno type="shelfmark">651/20</idno>
               <idno type="inventory">PbuG Inv. Nr. 18</idno>
               <altIdentifier type="former">
                  <idno>Deutsches Papyruskartell acquisition, 1907-1908</idno>
               </altIdentifier>
            </msIdentifier>
            <msContents>
               <summary>
                  <p>Bilingual Gothic-Latin Gospel book fragment. Preserves words from the Gospel of Luke: Latin text from verses 23:3-6 (f. 1r) and 24:5-9 (f. 8r); Gothic text from verses 23:11-14 (f. 1v) and 24:13, 15-17 (f. 8v). The Latin text belongs to the pre-Jerome tradition (Vetus Latina, ms. 36).</p>
               </summary>
               <msItem n="1">
                  <locus>ff. 1r-8v</locus>
                  <title type="uniform" xml:lang="en">Gospel of Luke</title>
                  <textLang mainLang="got" otherLangs="lat">Gothic and Latin</textLang>
               </msItem>
            </msContents>
            <physDesc>
               <objectDesc form="codex">
                  <supportDesc material="perg">
                     <support>
                        <material>Parchment</material>
                     </support>
                     <extent>
                        <measure type="dimensions" unit="mm">155 × 125 mm (fragment)</measure>
                        <p>Fragment originally formed the upper central part of the outer bifolium of a quire. Original page dimensions estimated at ca. 260-270 × 210-220 mm, with a written area of ca. 190-200 × 150-160 mm. Eleven lines survive out of 24-25 estimated.</p>
                     </extent>
                     <condition>
                        <p>Fragment lost since 1945. Only photographic reproductions survive. The fragment was stored in the Dresdner Bank vault in Giessen and lost after flooding of the River Lahn.</p>
                     </condition>
                  </supportDesc>
                  <layoutDesc>
                     <layout columns="2" writtenLines="24 25">
                        <p>Bifolium layout with Gothic text on left page (position of honor) and Latin text on right. Text laid out per cola et commata. Ruling executed on flesh side with double vertical bounding lines. Written area delimited by vertical ruling lines, with space between first and second lines measuring approximately two to three letters width.</p>
                     </layout>
                  </layoutDesc>
               </objectDesc>
               <handDesc hands="2">
                  <handNote xml:id="hand1" scope="major" script="Gothic">Gothic text written in vertical Gothic script. Use of Latin-form &lt;s&gt; links this manuscript to other Ostrogothic codices, suggesting Italian origin.</handNote>
                  <handNote xml:id="hand2" scope="major" script="uncial">Latin text written in uncial script 'not of the oldest type' (CLA VIII, 1200). Presumably of Italian origin. Uncertain whether manuscript copied by single bilingual scribe or two different scribes.</handNote>
               </handDesc>
               <scriptDesc>
                  <p>In Latin section, slightly larger letter module marks beginning of textual units, projecting beyond written area; Eusebian section numbers in smaller module. In some Gothic lines, letters at line end reduced in size and more closely spaced. Both scripts conform to well-established norms.</p>
               </scriptDesc>
               <decoDesc>
                  <decoNote type="rubrication">Two different inks used. Beginnings of Eusebian sections in Latin text are faded, suggesting use of distinctive ink (possibly red), less durable than darker ink for remainder of text. Paragraph marks in left margin at beginning of Eusebian sections: thin horizontal stroke thickened at ends and bisected by oblique stroke.</decoNote>
               </decoDesc>
               <additions>
                  <p>Traces of running title detected in upper margin of f. 1v (Gothic side), though some uncertainty remains.</p>
                  <p>Eusebian sections marked in left margin of Latin text, written in smaller module.</p>
                  <p>Numerous probationes pennae written at indeterminate period visible within fragment.</p>
               </additions>
            </physDesc>
            <history>
               <origin>
                  <origDate notBefore="0500" notAfter="0550">First third/first half of the sixth century</origDate>
                  <origPlace>Likely copied in Ostrogothic milieu or derived from Ostrogothic exemplar. Latin script presumably of Italian origin.</origPlace>
               </origin>
               <provenance notBefore="1907" notAfter="1908">
                  <p>Found in Egypt under unknown circumstances, possibly from Shêkh 'Abâde near Antinoe. Acquired 1907-1908 by Deutsches Papyruskartell. Various hypotheses regarding connection to place of discovery: pilgrim travel, use in Arian Church of Vandal kingdom, arrival with Amalafrida (sister of Theoderic, wife of Thrasamund), or Ostrogothic settlements in Egypt during/after Greco-Gothic War.</p>
               </provenance>
               <acquisition when="1945">
                  <p>Fragment stored in Dresdner Bank vault in Giessen with other manuscripts. Lost in 1945 following flooding of River Lahn. Unlike other manuscripts (which retained at least material support), all traces completely lost.</p>
               </acquisition>
            </history>
            <additional>
               <surrogates>
                  <p>Photographic reproductions prepared by Paul Glaue and Karl Helm (editio princeps, 1910). Digital images available at: <ref target="http://bibd.uni-giessen.de/papyri/images/pbug-inv018-1.jpg">http://bibd.uni-giessen.de/papyri/images/pbug-inv018-1.jpg</ref> and <ref target="http://bibd.uni-giessen.de/papyri/images/pbug-inv018-2.jpg">http://bibd.uni-giessen.de/papyri/images/pbug-inv018-2.jpg</ref>
                  </p>
               </surrogates>
